<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/expert/bioinformatics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the computational summit of the Biology track. The bench techniques — PCR, gels,
    sequencing reads — came from <em>Molecular Biology Techniques</em> (advanced); the tree
    thinking from <em>Speciation and Phylogenetics</em> (advanced); and the molecule itself,
    DNA as information, from <em>DNA and Heredity</em> (foundational). Here the subject stops
    being sequences on a gel and becomes sequences as data, at industrial scale.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>read a genetic-engineering project as an information pipeline — find, design, simulate, verify — with the wet lab as its last mile;</li>
    <li>interpret Phred quality scores and explain why pipelines filter on them before any biology is attempted;</li>
    <li>explain BLAST's seed-and-extend trade and read an E-value instead of trusting the top hit;</li>
    <li>describe assembly as graph theory over reads and annotation as machine learning;</li>
    <li>connect phylogenetic trees to outbreak surveillance, and AlphaFold to the 50-year structure problem.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Bioinformatics is <strong>biology's data discipline</strong>: algorithms, statistics and
    databases applied to sequences, structures and molecular measurements. Born of necessity
    (genomes outgrew notebooks), it now leads — AI systems predict structures, scan diagnoses
    from tumour genomes, and trace pandemics variant by variant in real time. The syllabus
    gives it one topic — biotechnology and genetic modification — and the expert reading of
    that topic is the perfect frame: genetic engineering is <em>information processing</em>,
    reading DNA, editing it and writing it, and every step of that pipeline is computational
    before it is biological.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pipeline from raw sequence to biological insight" widget="equationStep" config={bioinfCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are stations in one pipeline, and each
    hands a different artefact to the next. Card 1 is the firehose itself — terabytes of
    text arriving daily; card 2 turns raw text into comparisons; card 3 turns fragments into
    a labelled genome; card 4 turns sequence into predicted three-dimensional structure.
    Watch what changes hands at each station and how much of the work is already computation
    before any organism is touched — the pipeline's first ninety percent is code.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> bioinformatics is the discipline biology grew when its data
    outgrew its notebooks. The expert reading of biotechnology follows the pipeline
    end to end — starting with what genetic modification looks like when it is treated as
    information engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Biotechnology and genetic modification as information engineering</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's topic (21.1) is usually taught through its products: bacteria making
    insulin, crops with inserted genes. The bioinformatic view sits underneath: every
    genetic modification is a data operation. To insert a gene you must first <em>find</em>
    its sequence (databases: GenBank, Ensembl — gigabytes of annotated text that are the
    field's periodic table), <em>design</em> the construct (restriction-site planning, codon
    optimisation — choosing synonymous codons the host organism prefers, a translation-speed
    optimisation run in software), <em>simulate</em> the cloning (plasmid-design tools
    assemble the circular construct in silico before any enzyme touches a tube), and
    <em>verify</em> the result by sequencing the edited genome and computationally
    aligning it back to the plan. CRISPR deepened the computational dependence: guide-RNA
    design is a search for 20-base targets with minimal off-target similarity — a fuzzy
    string-matching problem run against the whole genome, scored and ranked by software
    before a pipette moves. The wet lab is the last mile of a pipeline whose first ninety
    percent is code.
  </p>

  <h2>Sequencing: turning DNA into text at scale</h2>
  <p>
    The data firehose begins with the sequencers. Sanger sequencing (1977) read one strand
    at a time and powered the Human Genome Project — 13 years, about 3 billion dollars for
    the draft. Second-generation machines (Illumina) read millions of fragments in
    parallel by photographing bases as they incorporate: a human genome in days for a few
    hundred to a thousand dollars, and the price curve is still falling. Third-generation
    platforms (PacBio, Oxford Nanopore) read single long molecules — tens of thousands of
    bases per read — which untangles repeats that shred short-read data. The output is
    text: FASTA files of A, C, G, T, one human genome about 100 GB once processed. Biology
    became a data science by arithmetic, and the sequencer is the sensor; everything
    downstream — alignment, assembly, interpretation — is computer science with a biology
    constraint attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    The data carry their own quality metadata, and reading it is a first-day skill.
    Each base arrives with a Phred score — Q = −10 log₁₀(p), where p is the estimated
    probability that the call is wrong — so Q30 means 99.9% accuracy, Q40 means
    one error in ten thousand. Pipelines filter and trim on these scores before any
    biology is attempted, because garbage bases propagate: a single bad base in an
    assembly read can misjoin contigs, and a miscount in an RNA-seq library shifts a
    gene's measured expression. Replicates, controls (spike-ins for RNA-seq) and
    batch-design discipline complete the foundation — the field's version of
    laboratory technique, executed in configuration files.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the genome is a blueprint — read it and you can build
    the organism." A blueprint is a one-to-one plan; a genome is closer to a recipe
    embedded in a self-assembling chemistry: genes interact, most regulatory logic lives
    outside genes, identical twins diverge, and the same genome produces a caterpillar
    and then a butterfly. Annotation pipelines encode the honest version: they label
    <em>candidate</em> functions and regulatory <em>potential</em>, not a determinism.
    The field's own history enforced this — humans have about 20,000 protein-coding
    genes, roughly the count in a worm; complexity lives in the network, not the parts
    count.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the sequencer is the sensor and its output is text — but text
    with quality metadata attached, which is what keeps the firehose honest. The first thing
    any pipeline does with that text is compare it: alignment, the field's workhorse
    algorithm, is next.
  </p>

  <h2>Alignment: the field's workhorse algorithm</h2>
  <p>
    Sequence alignment is the discipline's core computation: line up two sequences to find
    shared ancestry in shared order. The rigorous solution is dynamic programming —
    Needleman–Wunsch (1970) for global alignment, Smith–Waterman (1981) for local —
    filling a scoring grid where each cell is the best score ending there, with penalties
    for mismatches and gaps. The problem is scale: comparing one sequence against a
    billion-base database by the rigorous method would take years, so BLAST (1990) made
    the engineering trade — find short exact "seed" matches first (they are cheap), then
    extend only the promising ones. It is a heuristic, and it is the most-cited paper in
    biology's literature, with a web server that has been the field's front door for three
    decades. What alignment buys: conserved regions across species (the slower a region
    changes, the more essential its function), disease mutations pinned to functional
    domains, and phylogenetic trees — alignment is where sequence becomes evolutionary
    inference.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "BLAST finds <em>the</em> answer — the database search
    returns truth." BLAST returns heuristically selected alignments scored against a
    model; the raw output includes an E-value (how many hits of this quality you would
    expect by chance in a database that size), and reading that number correctly — not
    just taking the top hit — is the actual skill. Hits with E-values near 1 may be pure
    noise; a "significant" hit to the wrong database region sends a pipeline entirely
    astray. The tool narrows the search; the biologist decides.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> alignment turns similarity into inference — conservation,
    disease mutations, phylogenies — but it assumes you already have intact sequences to
    line up. The sequencer never gives you those; it gives fragments. Stitching them back
    together is the next station's problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Assembly and annotation: from reads to a genome</h2>
  <p>
    Sequencers do not read genomes; they read fragments, and assembly stitches them — a
    jigsaw with billions of pieces, no box lid, and stretches (repeats) where all pieces
    look identical. Short-read assemblers solve it with graph theory: break reads into
    k-mers, build a de Bruijn graph of overlaps, and walk the graph into contigs; long
    reads bridge the repeats and close the gaps. The first human "complete" genome was
    actually a mosaic of hundreds of gaps until the Telomere-to-Telomere consortium used
    long reads to finish one in 2022 — the last centromeres and satellite arrays, unreadable
    for twenty years, finally resolved. Annotation then labels the assembly: which
    stretches are genes, which regulatory elements, which selfish repeats (about half the
    human genome). Gene-finding was the first large-scale machine-learning problem in
    biology, and today's annotators — including tools that predict gene structure and
    function directly from sequence with neural networks — descend from that lineage.
  </p>

  <h2>Phylogenetics and outbreak surveillance: evolution as an algorithm</h2>
  <p>
    Comparative genomics scales alignment into <strong>phylogenetics</strong>: build the
    tree of relationships from shared mutations, assuming (with care) that fewer
    differences mean more recent common ancestry. This is where bioinformatics changed
    public health visibly: during the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic, millions of viral genomes were
    sequenced and shared openly, and phylogenetic pipelines placed each new sample on the
    evolving tree within days — naming the variants, tracing transmission chains,
    distinguishing reinfection from persistent infection, and guiding vaccine updates.
    The same tree-building reads deep history: it settled that whales sit inside the
    even-toed ungulates, that HIV jumped from chimpanzees early in the twentieth century,
    and that our own Neanderthal ancestry is real (a few percent of non-African genomes,
    found in the alignments). Evolution, famously described as the science with no
    experiments, turns out to be running a continuous data stream — and the tree is its
    regression model.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a phylogenetic tree is the family tree of organisms,
    read directly from fossils and guesses." Every published tree is a computed estimate
    from aligned sequence data under a model of molecular evolution — different models
    and gene choices give different trees, and genes themselves have histories that can
    differ from species histories (horizontal gene transfer in bacteria scrambles the
    picture entirely; mitochondria and chloroplasts carry their own genomes from
    different ancestors). The tree is an inference with quantified confidence, and
    "the" tree of life is a consensus of thousands of gene trees — some of which
    disagree productively.
  </p>

  <h2>Structure prediction: the 50-year problem, met</h2>
  <p>
    Protein structure prediction was biology's grand challenge: a protein's 3-D shape (which
    determines its function) follows from its amino-acid sequence by physics — Anfinsen's
    1972 Nobel showed the fold self-assembles — but computing the fold from sequence
    resisted everyone for half a century. DeepMind's AlphaFold2 (CASP14, 2020) effectively
    solved single-chain prediction by learning from the 170,000 known structures, using
    attention networks that reason over residue pairs; its public database now holds
    200+ million predicted structures, essentially every catalogued protein. The 2024
    Nobel in chemistry went to its builders (Hassabis and Jumper, shared with David Baker
    for computational protein design). Honest boundaries remain: protein–protein and
    protein–ligand interactions, dynamics and disordered regions are harder; a structure
    prediction is a hypothesis, not a measurement. But the pipeline "sequence in, structure
    out, function proposed" is now the default first pass for every new gene — the field's
    clearest case of computation doing what labs used to.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What does a Phred score of Q30 mean, and why do pipelines filter and trim on quality scores before any biology is attempted?</li>
    <li>Your BLAST search returns a top hit with an E-value near 1. A labmate says "top hit means it is that gene". What is wrong with that reading, and what should be checked instead?</li>
    <li>Why did protein structure prediction resist solution for fifty years, and what did AlphaFold2 exploit that made it tractable?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Q30 means 99.9% base-call accuracy — the Phred score is
    Q = −10 log₁₀(p), where p is the estimated probability the call is wrong. Quality is
    handled first because garbage propagates: a single bad base can misjoin contigs in an
    assembly, and a miscount in an RNA-seq library shifts a gene's measured expression.
    (2) An E-value near 1 means hits of that quality are expected by chance in a database
    that size — the top hit may be pure noise, and rank alone is meaningless. What to check
    is the E-value itself, the database's appropriateness, and whether the alignment covers
    the right region: the tool narrows the search, but the biologist decides. (3) The fold
    self-assembles from the sequence by physics (Anfinsen's 1972 Nobel), yet computing the
    fold from sequence defeated everyone for half a century. AlphaFold2 learned from the
    170,000 known structures, using attention networks that reason over residue pairs — and
    the honest boundary survives: a structure prediction is a hypothesis, not a measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Genetic engineering is information processing — find, design, simulate, verify — and the wet lab is the last mile of the pipeline.</li>
    <li>Sequencing output is text with quality metadata; Phred scores gate everything downstream.</li>
    <li>BLAST's seed-and-extend trade made search tractable; the E-value, not the top rank, is the number to read.</li>
    <li>Assembly is graph theory over k-mers, with long reads bridging repeats — the last human gaps fell only in 2022.</li>
    <li>A phylogenetic tree is a computed estimate under a model of molecular evolution, not a read-off family tree.</li>
    <li>AlphaFold2 effectively solved single-chain structure prediction; its 200+ million structures are hypotheses at industrial scale.</li>
    <li>Databases and standards are the field's true instruments — and their biases have clinical consequences.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lessons pick up the two halves of this pipeline.
    <em>Synthetic Biology</em> takes the writing end — once reading, editing and designing
    DNA are computational, constructing whole genetic circuits becomes engineering. And
    <em>Systems Biology</em> takes the interpretation end — from annotated parts lists to
    the networks and dynamics those parts make together.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Omics and the integration problem.</strong> Genomics is one layer of a stack:
    transcriptomics (which genes are switched on, measured by RNA-seq), proteomics (which
    proteins are present, by mass spectrometry), epigenomics (which regions are methylated
    or open), metabolomics (the chemical state). Each layer is its own data discipline with
    its own biases — RNA-seq needs normalisation and differential-expression statistics
    (which genes changed <em>more than measurement noise</em> is a hypothesis test per
    gene, times twenty thousand, demanding false-discovery-rate control); proteomics is
    inference from peptide fragments. The frontier is multi-omic integration: single-cell
    methods measuring RNA in thousands of individual cells reveal that tissues are
    mosaics of states, and linking genetic variants to expression (eQTLs) to disease is
    the current route from correlation toward mechanism. The statistics is genuinely hard,
    and "we found a significant gene" without multiple-testing correction is the field's
    most common published sin.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Data infrastructure as scientific instrument.</strong> The field's unglamorous
    core is databases and standards: GenBank/ENA/DDBJ sharing sequence data across borders
    by treaty-like agreement; the FASTA and FASTQ formats as de facto law; versioned
    reference genomes (GRCh38) that every pipeline pins to; FAIR principles (findable,
    accessible, interoperable, reusable) governing how data are deposited. During the
    pandemic, open sharing of SARS-CoV-2 genomes via GISAID and then open repositories was
    arguably the single highest-leverage scientific act of the event — the surveillance
    above was possible because thousands of labs uploaded, unembargoed. The inverse lesson
    also lands: data hoarding, format drift and unmigrated servers have silently destroyed
    decades of irreplaceable biological data — reproducibility in this field is an
    engineering problem before it is a scientific virtue.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier.</strong> Foundation models for biology (protein language models
    trained on hundreds of millions of sequences, learning structure and function
    implicitly), generative protein design (specify a fold, synthesise the gene that
    makes it), personal-genome pipelines in the clinic (the genomic-medicine page's
    subject), and pathogen-genomic early warning as permanent public infrastructure. The
    ethical edges sharpen accordingly: privacy of sequence data (it identifies relatives,
    not just individuals — the Golden State Killer case was solved by genealogy databases),
    consent for secondary uses, and equity in whose genomes populate the reference
    databases (African genomes are the most diverse and the least represented — a
    measurement bias with clinical consequences). The field that began as a way to store
    sequences now sets the terms for what biology's next century of experiments will be.
  </p>
